\section{Define What the Tracer Is Allowed to Miss}

Incremental tracing runs short collection slices separated by
mutator execution. Concurrent tracing permits the tracer and
mutator to execute at the same time. Both see a changing heap;
the concurrent design must additionally handle interleavings
within a scan or a store. In tricolor terminology, white
objects are undiscovered, gray objects are queued or being
scanned, and black objects have been scanned. These are
logical states, not necessarily three header bits. Appel
uses the colors to describe barrier obligations
\cite{appel2004modern}; Moon describes incremental copying
in a real Lisp system. Here keep the heap nonmoving, so the
first problem is preserving reachability rather than making
all loads tolerate evacuation.

Imagine \(B\) is black and \(G\) is gray. The only path to
white \(W\) currently goes through \(G\). The program copies
the edge into \(B\), then deletes it from \(G\). If the tracer
never inspects \(B\) again and later scans the cleared field
in \(G\), it can miss the reachable \(W\). \Cref{fig:visual-416}
shows what an insertion barrier must notice. Marking longer
without a barrier does not make this race less fundamental.

