\section{Start With Serialized Incremental Slices}

\begin{verbatim}
begin_mark():
    stop_mutator()
    enable_mark_epoch()
    for root in roots: shade(root.load())
    resume_mutator()

store_pointer(slot, value):
    if marking: shade(value)
    slot.store(value)

mark_slice(budget):
    stop_mutator()
    while budget > 0 and gray_queue not empty:
        x := gray_queue.pop()
        for slot in strong_slots(x): shade(slot.load())
        blacken(x)
        budget -= 1
    if gray_queue empty:
        for root in roots: shade(root.load())
        if gray_queue empty: finish_mark_and_sweep()
    resume_mutator()
\end{verbatim}

Here shading enqueues a white object once. The insertion
barrier conservatively shades all new heap targets during
marking, not only those written by black holders. New objects
start black with empty slots; initialization writes obey the
same barrier. Root slots are enumerated at start and checked
again before ending the mark, allowing the ordinary mutator
registers to change between slices. The example excludes
weak-reference upgrades and other operations that can newly
expose white objects; add appropriate barriers before
introducing them. No mutator runs at the same time as a
slice, so there is no race between field loads and shading.
The budget counts objects for clarity, but scanning one large
reference vector can exceed a useful pause budget. Chunk
long scans and leave continuation work visible in the queue.

\section{Move From Slices to Concurrent Publication}

Concurrent tracing needs synchronized mark transitions and
slot reads that respect the host memory model. One thread
may change a mark bit while another is about to queue the
same object. Duplicate queue entries are often cheaper to
tolerate than lost work, but if only the winner of an atomic
mark transition can enqueue, other threads must account
for the interval between winning and publishing the work.
Changing a mark bit does not publish the object's descriptor
or make its fields safe to read; allocation needs its own
initialization and publication protocol.

An empty shared queue is not termination. A worker may be
scanning an object, a mutator may hold a partially filled
barrier buffer, or a winning marker may not yet have published
its item. A simple finish protocol stops mutators, waits for
in-flight barrier operations, flushes local logs, and brings
workers to a known state. Then it drains all work and scans
the necessary roots before reclaiming. More concurrent
termination is possible, but the protocol must account for
the same sources of work. Reclaiming concurrently with
mutators after marking adds another set of synchronization
requirements between sweeping, buffer retirement, and
allocators; do not infer them from the marking protocol.

\section{Compare Marking Semantics and Scheduling Costs}

A snapshot-at-the-beginning barrier logs the previous
referent of an overwritten reference so marking can
preserve the initial reachable graph even after deletion.
Its proof differs from the insertion policy: it retains
some objects no longer reachable at the end. New allocations
must be treated as live for that cycle, and initial roots
need a well-defined snapshot. Use the actual replaced value,
not an unrelated load raced by another writer. A collector
can intentionally retain extra objects under either policy.
That floating garbage is not a safety bug, but increases
the memory headroom needed while a cycle is running.

Measure mutator barrier cost, allocation rate, marking
throughput, and final-pause work separately. If marking falls
behind the allocation rate, it may need mutator assists or
earlier starting thresholds. Parallel workers can still
lose time to shared queue contention or cache-line traffic.
To isolate correctness, force the store sequence in the
figure between two mark slices, then test the concurrent
version with a worker paused after claiming a mark and before
publishing it. Force a finish request while a barrier buffer
has exactly one entry. Tests with no precise control over
scheduling may never exercise those boundary states.

\section{Keep Compiler Ordering Part of the Contract}

Once generated code emits a barrier inline, ordinary field
stores and barrier operations cannot be moved independently.
An optimizer might otherwise schedule an insertion barrier
after a safepoint to improve register allocation, or remove
an apparently redundant old-value load used by a deletion
barrier. Represent the barrier's effects in the IR or lower
its parts together after the relevant optimization passes.
Audit atomic fields and bulk copies too: instructions that
update several slots need coverage for each relevant edge.
A kernel memory barrier alone does not discover an edge; a
collector write barrier describes a semantic action and may
in turn need memory ordering to carry it out safely.

\section{State the Completion Argument at a Fixed Heap State}

For the serialized insertion design, assume every black object
has no white strong target when a pause starts. Scanning a
gray object shades all its targets before turning it black,
so that step preserves the property. Between pauses a store
cannot create a black-to-white edge because its new target
was shaded before installing the edge. At completion, the
mutator is stopped, every root has a nonwhite target, and the
gray queue is empty. If a root could reach a white object,
take the first white object on that path. It would either
be a root target or have a black predecessor, both excluded.
This proof says why an empty queue has meaning only at the
stated boundary. It does not say every black object is still
reachable; black can include floating garbage.

When adapting the argument to concurrency, deliberately pause a
worker after it reads a reference from one vector slot but
before it reads the next. Replace both slots from the mutator.
Independently test a root change at the last handshake.
The mark state and queued barrier work together must justify
the final fixed heap argument; a collection cannot interpret
the last queue read as proof that no more work will arrive.
